According to the provided excerpt, preregistration is similar to a dissertation proposal where you lay out your research plan in advance [1]. This helps ensure that you don't change your methods after the study is completed [1]. The excerpt also mentions "registered reports," which is preregistration done with a specific journal with a publication agreement [1].



To learn more about Qualitative Open Science Practices, you can visit: [https://forrt.org/educators-corner/006-qualitative-os-practices/](https://forrt.org/educators-corner/006-qualitative-os-practices/)

]
ReproCrowd is a crowdsourced platform for numerical reproductions that allows you to check the statistics reported in a paper for errors [1]. You can use the ReproCrowd app to rebuild a paper's analysis from its procedures, run it on the published data, and check if the numbers reproduce [1]. The process involves choosing an article, running the reproducibility check, checking and signing the results, and sharing and comparing the findings [1].

The platform provides a desktop app for AI-assisted computational reproductions that you can [Download the latest release](https://github.com/forrtproject/reprocrowd-app/releases) or [View source on GitHub](https://github.com/forrtproject/reprocrowd-app) [1].
